------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This software also uses a number of additional open-source Rust crates from
the broader ecosystem, distributed under permissive licenses (predominantly
MIT and/or Apache-2.0; some BSD/ISC). A full machine-readable inventory of
the dependency tree and its licenses can be produced with `cargo metadata`
or `cargo about`. Notable ones include: tokio, anyhow, thiserror, tracing,
bytes, clap, mp4, matroska-demuxer, libloading, tempfile (MIT/Apache-2.0);
rubato (MIT, sample-rate conversion), md5 (MIT/Apache-2.0, through
rivet-lossless); moxcms (BSD-3-Clause OR Apache-2.0, ICC colour management
for still images, with the `image` feature; not a codec). Every audio, video
and still-image codec rivet itself decodes or encodes in software is one of
the original crates above, except JPEG XL decoding, which is jxl-rs (above).
No other third-party codec library or crate is used:
libopus / audiopus, minimp3, lewton and the run-time-loaded LAME were removed
on 2026-10-03, and so were rav1e, rav1d, ravif, the `image` crate (with its
png, jpeg-decoder, gif, tiff and WebP decoders), jpeg-encoder, the webp crate
with Google's libwebp, and openh264 (Cisco's H.264 decoder, which was the
optional `openh264-fallback` tier). No build of rivet links FFmpeg or any
of its libraries.

